[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"State Synchronization, RPCs, and Prediction/Reconciliation in Unity"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Building a responsive multiplayer game requires mastering three core networking concepts: State Synchronization, Remote Procedure Calls (RPCs), and Client-Side Prediction with Server Reconciliation. This tutorial explores how to implement these patterns in Unity using a generic authoritative networking model."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Understanding the Core Concepts"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"State Synchronization: The server continuously or periodically broadcasts the authoritative state (positions, health, variables) of game objects to all connected clients."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Remote Procedure Calls (RPCs): Functions executed across the network. They are ideal for discrete, non-continuous events like firing a weapon, playing a sound, or triggering an animation."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Client-Side Prediction & Reconciliation: To hide network latency, the client immediately applies local inputs to simulate movement locally (prediction). When the authoritative server state arrives, the client corrects any discrepancies (reconciliation)."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Implementing Client-Side Prediction for Movement"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"The following script demonstrates how a player controller can predict its own movement locally while stamping inputs with a sequence number for the server to process."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"using UnityEngine;

public class PlayerController : MonoBehaviour
{
    public struct PlayerInput
    {
        public int sequenceNumber;
        public Vector2 axisInput;
        public float timestamp;
    }

    private int currentSequence = 0;
    private float moveSpeed = 5.0f;

    void Update()
    {
        if (!IsOwner()) return;

        Vector2 input = new Vector2(Input.GetAxis("Horizontal"), Input.GetAxis("Vertical"));
        if (input.sqrMagnitude > 0)
        {
            PlayerInput currentInput = new PlayerInput
            {
                sequenceNumber = ++currentSequence,
                axisInput = input,
                timestamp = Time.time
            };

            ApplyMovement(currentInput.axisInput);
            SendToServer(currentInput);
        }
    }

    void ApplyMovement(Vector2 input)
    {
        transform.Translate(new Vector3(input.x, 0, input.y) * moveSpeed * Time.deltaTime);
    }

    bool IsOwner() { return true; } // Placeholder for network ownership check
    void SendToServer(PlayerInput input) { }
}"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Server Reconciliation Pattern"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"When the server processes the input, it computes the authoritative position and sends it back. If the client's predicted position diverges significantly from the server state, reconciliation snaps or smoothly interpolates the character back to the correct position."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"public void ReconcileState(Vector3 serverPosition, int lastProcessedSequence)
{
    float threshold = 0.5f;
    if (Vector3.Distance(transform.position, serverPosition) > threshold)
    {
        // Snap or smoothly correct to server position
        transform.position = serverPosition;

        // Re-apply unacknowledged inputs here if necessary
    }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Utilizing RPCs for Discrete Events"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"For actions that do not require continuous synchronization, such as weapon firing, use RPCs. Ensure that validation logic always runs on the server to prevent cheating."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"// Pseudo-code attribute representation for RPCs
// [ServerRpc]
publiciyaç void CmdFireWeapon(Vector3 origin, Vector3 direction)
{
    // Server-side validation
    if (HasAmmo())
    {
        ConsumeAmmo();
        RpcClientFired(origin, direction);
    }
}

// [ClientRpc]
public void RpcClientFired(Vector3 origin, Vector3 direction)
{
    // Play muzzle flash and spawn projectile visually on all clients
    Debug.Log("Weapon fired across the network!");
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"By combining state synchronization for passive objects, RPCs for discrete triggers, and client-side prediction with reconciliation for fast-paced player movement, you can build a robust, low-latency multiplayer architecture in Unity."}]
